      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "We begin by factoring $\\cos$ BMB's answer using the cosine rule, $BS^2=AB^2+MC^2$:\n\n\\[ BS^2=AB^2+3AC^2. \\]\n\nThe minimum value of $BS$ is when $L\\perp BS$, which places $BS$ on the radical axis of the circumcircles of $\\triangle AEL$, $\\triangle BDC$, and $\\triangle ABD$. Using this fact, we can deduce that $AB = BDC = AEB = 6$, meaning $\\angle BSA + \\angle BAS = \\angle BSA + \\angle BCA = 90^\\circ$. Therefore, we have $\\angle BAS = \\angle BCA$ and must have $\\angle BDA = \\angle BAD$. Consequently, $OS = BS \\sin \\angle BSA \\leq BS\\sin 30^\\circ = \\dfrac{1}{2}BS$. Since $AB=AC$, we can see that $AH = OI$, where $I$ is the intersection of lines $DA$ and $BS$. Now, $OM = \\dfrac{AB+AC-BC}{2} = 2 \\implies \\dfrac{SI}{SI+OI+AI}=1= \\dfrac{OP}{IB+OI+CS}$, so $SI = OI + CS = OI$.\n\nAnswer is option A.",
